```python
import math

def validate(record, schema):
    bad = []
    for field, rule in schema.items():
        value = record.get(field)
        if rule["type"] == "number":
            ok = (isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool)
                  and not math.isnan(value) and rule["min"] <= value <= rule["max"])
        else:
            ok = value in rule["allowed"]
        if not ok:
            bad.append(field)
    return sorted(bad)
```

The edge cases are the real lesson. A boolean passes as an integer in Python unless you exclude it; NaN slips through type checks and makes every comparison false (so `NaN <= 300` is false, which this code relies on only after checking `isnan` explicitly); a string that looks like a number (`"18.5"`) must be rejected, not silently converted, or a bug upstream goes unnoticed. In production the same rules are usually declared once in a schema library and applied both to the training data and to every live request, so the two cannot disagree.
